.TH MAIL_FILTER_RULES.CONF 5 "August 2026" "mail_filter 1.0.4" "File Formats"
.nh
.SH NAME
mail_filter_rules.conf \- policy rules for mail_filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP 40
.I /usr/local/etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_rules.conf
FreeBSD
.TP
.I /etc/mail_filter/mail_filter_rules.conf
Linux
.SH DESCRIPTION
The rules file defines policy rules that
.BR mail_filter (8)
evaluates for each incoming message.
.PP
Every rule below can test any combination of the decoded Subject header,
the raw message body, the envelope sender (MAIL FROM), envelope
recipients (RCPT TO), and any message header including
.IR From ,
.IR To ,
.IR Cc ,
.I Reply-To
and arbitrary
.I X\-headers
\(em in the same rule, if needed. This makes it possible to write a
single rule such as \(lqreject if the Subject looks like an invoice AND
the body asks for a wire transfer\(rq.
.PP
Conditions are composed with the infix logical operators
.BR and ,
.BR or ,
and
.BR not ,
with parentheses used for grouping \(em similar to Python or most
general-purpose languages.
The legacy function-style combinators
.BR anyof( ) ,
.BR allof( ) ,
and
.BR not( )
(as used by the Sieve mail filtering language) remain fully supported
and may be freely mixed with the new operators; see
.B "LOGICAL OPERATORS"
below.
.PP
Rules are evaluated top-to-bottom.
Evaluation stops at the first
.B reject
or
.B accept
action.
If no rule matches the message is accepted silently.
.SH FILE FORMAT
Lines that are empty or begin with
.B #
are ignored.
Rules are written as
.B if
statements with
.BR {  }
blocks.
.PP
.nf
.RS
if \fIcondition\fR {
    \fIaction\fR;
}

if \fIcondition\fR {
    \fIaction\fR;
} else if \fIcondition\fR {
    \fIaction\fR;
} else {
    \fIaction\fR;
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Blocks may contain further
.B if
statements (arbitrary nesting).
.SH LOGICAL OPERATORS
.SS Infix operators (recommended)
.TP
.I cond1 \fBand\fI cond2\fR
True only if \fIboth\fR conditions are true.
.TP
.I cond1 \fBor\fI cond2\fR
True if \fIeither\fR condition is true.
.TP
.BI not\ cond
Negates \fIcond\fR.
Parentheses around \fIcond\fR are optional unless needed for grouping
(see precedence below); e.g. both
.I "not envelope_from /pattern/i"
and
.I "not(envelope_from /pattern/i)"
are valid and equivalent.
.PP
\fBand\fR, \fBor\fR and \fBnot\fR may be chained and combined freely, e.g.:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if subject /pattern/i and not envelope_from /pattern2/i {
    reject;
}

if subject /a/i and not (envelope_from /x/i or envelope_from /y/i) {
    reject;
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Operator precedence
From highest to lowest:
.B not
\(rs
.B and
\(rs
.BR or .
This is the same convention used by Python and most languages, so
.RS
.I "a or b and not c"
.RE
parses as
.RS
.I "a or (b and (not c))" .
.RE
Use parentheses whenever the intended grouping is not obvious \(em
this is a common source of subtle rule bugs.
For example,
.RS
.I "subject /x/i or subject /y/i and envelope_from /z/i"
.RE
means \(lqreject if x, OR if (y AND z)\(rq \(em it does
.B NOT
require \fIz\fR when \fIx\fR matches.
If the intent was \(lqx or y, and always require z\(rq, parentheses
are required:
.RS
.I "(subject /x/i or subject /y/i) and envelope_from /z/i"
.RE
.SS Legacy function-style combinators
The original Sieve-like syntax remains fully supported and is
parsed identically to the equivalent infix expression:
.TP
.BI anyof( cond ", " cond ", " \fR...\fR )
True if \fIat least one\fR child condition is true (logical OR).
Requires at least two arguments.
Equivalent to chaining the children with
.BR or .
.TP
.BI allof( cond ", " cond ", " \fR...\fR )
True if \fIall\fR child conditions are true (logical AND).
Requires at least two arguments.
Equivalent to chaining the children with
.BR and .
.TP
.BI not( cond )
Negates the child condition.
Equivalent to
.I "not cond"
in the infix syntax.
.PP
Because
.B anyof(...)
and
.B allof(...)
are ordinary primaries in the condition grammar, they may be freely
mixed with
.BR and / or / not
and with parenthesized sub-expressions, e.g.:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if allof(
    subject /invoice/i,
    not anyof(
        rcpt_to /^<accounting@example\.com>$/i,
        rcpt_to /^<director@example\.com>$/i
    )
) {
    reject "Invoice: unauthorized recipient";
}

\fR\(em is exactly equivalent to \(em

if subject /invoice/i and not (
    rcpt_to /^<accounting@example\.com>$/i or rcpt_to /^<director@example\.com>$/i
) {
    reject "Invoice: unauthorized recipient";
}
.RE
.fi
There is no functional difference between the two forms; pick
whichever reads more clearly for a given rule.
.B allof/anyof
tend to read better for long lists of conditions, while
.B and/or/not
tend to read better for short, natural-language-like expressions.
.SH CONDITIONS
.SS Test keywords
Each test consists of a keyword followed by either an inline
.I /regex/flags
literal or a
.BI refile: path
reference to a file containing regex literals.
The pattern is applied with
.I re.search()
so it need not be anchored.
The
.B refile:
form is accepted by every regex-based test described below, including
.B header \(dqName\(dq.
.TP
.BI subject " /pattern/flags"
Match the decoded Subject header.
RFC\~2047 encoded-words (=?UTF-8?Q?...?=) are decoded to Unicode
before the pattern is tested.
.TP
.BI body " /pattern/flags"
Match the raw message body as UTF-8 text.
Only the first
.B max_body_kb
kilobytes of the body are retained and tested; see
.BR mail_filter.conf (5).
Before matching, quoted-printable soft line breaks
.RI ( "=\e\enor=\er\en" )
are merged so that an entity or word artificially split across a QP
line boundary (e.g.\& \fI&#22=\er\en5;\fR) is rejoined into
\fI&#225;\fR first. The body is
.B not
MIME-decoded or HTML-stripped before matching \(em patterns are
matched against the wire-level text of the part(s) Postfix hands to
the milter, including any HTML markup and entities. Use this to
detect obfuscation techniques such as heavy HTML-entity encoding; see
.B SECTION 3
of the sample
.B mail_filter_rules.conf
for an example.
.TP
.BI envelope_from " /pattern/flags"
Match the SMTP MAIL FROM address (envelope sender) as supplied by Postfix.
The angle brackets are included, e.g.\&
.IR <sender@example.com> .
When anchoring with
.B $
remember the trailing
.BR > ,
e.g.
.I /@example\.com>$/i
or simply leave the pattern unanchored.
.TP
.BI envelope_to " /pattern/flags"
.TQ
.BI rcpt_to " /pattern/flags"
True if the pattern matches
.I any
envelope RCPT TO address.
Both keywords are synonymous.
The angle brackets are included, same as
.IR envelope_from .
.TP
.BI from " /pattern/flags"
Match the decoded and unfolded
.I From:
header value (the header, not the envelope sender). RFC 2047 encoded-words
are decoded before matching.
.TP
.BI to " /pattern/flags"
Match the decoded and unfolded
.I To:
header value.
.TP
.BI cc " /pattern/flags"
Match the decoded and unfolded
.I Cc:
header value.
.TP
.BI reply_to " /pattern/flags"
Match the decoded and unfolded
.I Reply-To:
header value.
.TP
.BI header " \(dqName\(dq /pattern/flags"
Match a named header.
The name is case-insensitive.
True if the pattern matches
.I any
occurrence when the header appears multiple times.
.SS Regex files (refile:)
Instead of an inline regex, any test may refer to a file of regular
expressions using:
.PP
.nf
.RS
subject refile:/etc/mail_filter/spam_subjects.regex
header "X-Spam-Status" refile:lists/spam-status.regex
.RE
.fi
.PP
A regex-file path may be absolute or relative.
A relative path is resolved relative to the directory containing the
main
.B mail_filter_rules.conf
file, not relative to the daemon's current working directory.
For example, if the rules file and
.I spam_subjects.regex
are in the same directory, the preferred compact form is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
subject refile:spam_subjects.regex
.RE
.fi
.PP
Absolute paths remain fully supported.
Paths containing whitespace are not supported.
.PP
The referenced file is UTF-8 text. Empty lines and lines whose first
non-whitespace character is
.B #
are ignored. Every other line must contain exactly one regex literal:
.PP
.nf
.RS
/pattern/flags
/another\es+pattern/i
/^prefix-/ms
.RE
.fi
.PP
The same flags
.BR i ,
.BR m ,
.BR s
and
.B x
are available as for inline regexes. Unicode escape preprocessing is
also identical.
.PP
Regex files are read and compiled when the rules are loaded. This occurs
at daemon startup, during
.B --configtest
and during a SIGUSR1 rules reload.
.PP
Regex files are not opened for each message. An empty regex file is valid
and never matches. An unreadable file, malformed line, or invalid regular
expression causes a rules parse error.
.PP
When a regex from a referenced file matches, logging and
.B --test
identify the referenced file, its line number, and the exact regex literal
that matched.
.SS Regex flags
Flags follow the closing
.B /
of the pattern with no spaces.
.TP
.B i
Case-insensitive.
.TP
.B m
Multiline:
.B ^
and
.B $
match at line boundaries within the value.
.TP
.B s
Dotall: the dot metacharacter matches newlines.
.TP
.B x
Verbose: unescaped whitespace and inline
.B #
comments inside the pattern are ignored.
.SS Unicode escape sequences in patterns
The milter preprocesses the following sequences before passing the
pattern to the regex engine, making it safe to specify code points
as ASCII text:
.TP
.BI \eu xxxx
Unicode code point (four hex digits, BMP only).
.TP
.BI \eU xxxxxxxx
Unicode code point (eight hex digits, required for supplementary
planes such as emoji and CJK Extension B).
.PP
Example:
.nf
.RS
subject /[\eu3040-\eu30FF\eu4E00-\eu9FFF]/  -- Japanese/Chinese
subject /[\eU0001F300-\eU0001F9FF]/          -- emoji
.RE
.fi
.SS Example using a regex file
With this rule:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if subject refile:spam_subjects.regex {
    reject "Spam";
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
and this contents of
.IR spam_subjects.regex :
.PP
.nf
.RS
/free money/i
/urgent\es+payment/i
/^cryptocurrency offer/
/Action Needed\es*:\es*Account (?:Inactive|Suspended|Locked)/i
/\ed+\es*%\es*(?:off|discount)/i
/Your (?:cloud|storage|account) (?:is|will be) (?:full|disabled|suspended)/i
/\e$\ed+(?:\e.\ed+)?\es+(?:reward|bonus|prize)/i
/^(?:FWD?|RE):\es*/i
/verify your account (?:now|immediately|today)/i
.RE
.fi
.PP
the condition is true if any one of the file's regexes matches the
decoded Subject. This is a realistic, if small, spam list: literal
phrases, flexible whitespace
.RB ( \es* ", " \es+ ),
digits
.RB ( \ed+ ),
an escaped dollar sign
.RB ( \e$ ),
an optional group
.RB ( (?:\e.\ed+)? " for a decimal part),"
alternation between wordings
.RB ( (?:a|b|c) ),
and an anchored prefix
.RB ( ^ ).
Combining a handful of these covers most of the wording spammers
vary between otherwise-identical messages.
.SS Building a regex-file list from real mail
A practical way to grow a list like the one above: collect the Subject
lines of messages you already know are spam (from a quarantine folder,
a training corpus, or your own mail_filter log), then generalize each
one into a pattern instead of matching it literally verbatim \(em
replace numbers with
.BR \ed+ ,
loosen spacing around punctuation to
.B \es*
or
.BR \es+ ,
and fold near-identical wordings into one
.B (?:a|b|c)
alternation rather than keeping several separate lines.
.PP
Before deploying a hand-edited or generated list, validate it against
a corpus of real Subject lines \(em both the spam you built it from and
some legitimate mail, so you can see false positives before your users
do:
.PP
.nf
.RS
check_regex.py known_spam_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex
check_regex.py known_spam_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex --show-found
check_regex.py known_good_subjects.txt spam_subjects.regex --reverse
.RE
.fi
.PP
The first form reports lines from
.I known_spam_subjects.txt
that no regex in the file matches (candidates for a new pattern);
.B --show-found
additionally prints what did match, and with which regex, so you can
spot a pattern that is broader than intended;
.B --reverse
inverts the logic, so running it against known-legitimate Subject
lines reports any that a spam pattern would incorrectly catch. See
.BR check_regex.py (1)
for the full option list. The companion tool
.BR generate_emoji_regex.py (1)
builds the same kind of refile list for a narrower, mechanically
generated case \(em Unicode emoji ranges \(em rather than free-text
wording; see its own man page for that one's workflow.
.SS A Python-regex gotcha coming from sed(1)/grep(1)
mail_filter compiles every pattern \(em inline or from a regex file
\(em with Python's
.B re
module, not POSIX regex. If you are used to writing
.BR sed (1)
or
.BR grep (1)
patterns, the POSIX bracket classes
.RB ( [:alpha:] ", " [:digit:] ", " [:space:] ,
and so on) do
.B not
work here: Python's
.B re
has no special meaning for them, so a rule written as
.I subject /[[:digit:]]/
will
.B never
match an actual digit \(em Python parses
.I [[:digit:]]
as an ordinary character class built from the literal characters
.IR [ ,
.IR : ,
.IR d ,
.IR i ,
.IR g
and
.I t
(closed by the first
.IR ] ),
followed by a stray literal
.I ]
in the pattern, and silently gives you that instead of an error.
(Python itself is suspicious enough of this to print
.I "FutureWarning: Possible nested set"
when the pattern is compiled \(em but nothing stops it from loading
into a rules file, where that warning is easy to miss.)
Use the Perl-style
equivalents instead, which Python's
.B re
does understand:
.BR \ed " for [:digit:], " \ew " for [:alnum:] plus underscore, " \es
for [:space:], and an explicit range like
.BR [a-zA-Z]
for [:alpha:]. All of these appear in the example list above.
.SS Numeric comparisons
Every test keyword documented above (including
.BR body )
also accepts a numeric comparison in place of a
.I /pattern/flags
or
.I refile:
source:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\fItest-keyword\fR [\(dqName\(dq] \fIop\fR \fInumber\fR
.RE
.fi
.PP
where
.I op
is one of
.BR > ,
.BR < ,
.BR >= ,
.BR <= ,
.BR == ,
or
.BR != ,
and
.I number
is an optionally-signed integer or decimal, e.g.
.IR 15 ,
.IR -3.2 ,
.IR 1000 .
This is primarily useful with
.B header
for testing a numeric header value such as a spam score, without having
to hand-write a regex that separately covers every possible digit count:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if header "X-Spam-Score-Total" > 15 {
    reject "Spam score too high";
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
The milter parses a leading, optionally-signed number out of the
field's value \(em ignoring anything that follows, so
.I \(dq17.20 required=5.0 tests=...\(dq
is read as
.I 17.2
\(em and compares it against
.I number
using
.IR op .
If the field's value does not begin with a number, the test is simply
false; it never raises an error or aborts rule evaluation.
.PP
A numeric test correctly matches values of any magnitude, unlike a regex
built for a specific digit count. For example
.I "> 15"
matches
.IR 16 ,
.IR 23.5 ,
and
.I 1000
(the fixed score SpamAssassin's GTUBE test string produces) alike, with
no risk of a pattern tuned for two-digit scores silently missing a
three-or-more-digit one.
.PP
Numeric and regex tests combine freely with
.BR and / or / not
and with regex or numeric tests on other fields, including
.BR body ,
e.g.:
.PP
.nf
.RS
if header "X-Spam-Score-Total" > 15
   and subject /invoice/i {
    reject "High-scoring message impersonating an invoice";
}
.RE
.fi
.SH ACTIONS
Actions appear inside
.B { }
blocks and must end with a semicolon.
.TP
.BI "reject " \(dqmessage\(dq ;
Reject the message with a permanent 550\~5.7.1 SMTP error.
The quoted message is sent to the client and written to the log.
The message text may not contain a bare double-quote character.
If the message is omitted a default text is used.
.TP
.B accept ;
Accept the message immediately; no further rules are evaluated.
Useful inside an
.B else
branch to explicitly pass a message that would otherwise fall
through to a later REJECT rule.
.TP
.BI "warn " \(dqmessage\(dq ;
Write a WARNING-level log entry and continue evaluating rules.
The message is delivered normally.
Useful for monitoring patterns before converting them to
.BR reject .
.TP
.B dunno ;
Continue evaluating the next rule without taking any action.
Equivalent to a false condition; useful inside
.B else
branches as an explicit no-op.
.SH BACKREFERENCES IN MESSAGES
A
.B reject
or
.B warn
message may pull in the regex's own capture groups, like Postfix's
.BR pcre_table (5):
.PP
.nf
.RS
if subject /name\es*=\es*"?([^;]*invoice[^;]*\e.(zip|rar))"?/i {
    reject "$1 attachment";
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B $1
,
.BR $2 ", etc." ,
substitute the matched capture groups;
.B $0
is the whole match.
.B $$
produces a literal
.BR $ .
Write
.B ${1}
instead of
.B $1
when the number would otherwise run into following text (e.g.
.BR id${1}9 ,
not
.BR id$19 ,
which would mean group 19). A capture group that did not participate in
the match substitutes as empty.
.PP
Since only one regex can unambiguously be "the" match, backreferences are
only allowed directly inside a single regex-based condition
.RI ( subject ,
.IR body ,
.IR envelope_from ,
.IR envelope_to ,
.IR from ,
.IR to ,
.IR cc ,
.IR reply_to ,
or
.IR header ),
or an
.B anyof(...)
of such conditions (the one that actually matched supplies the groups).
.B allof(...)
is rejected at load time \(em more than one condition could each
contribute groups, and it is not obvious whose
.B $1
would win. A negated
.B not(...)
condition is rejected too, matching Postfix's own restriction
(pcre_table(5): "substitutions are not available for negated patterns")
\(em by definition it has no match to take groups from. A numeric
comparison
.RI ( "e.g. body_size > 100000" )
is rejected as well; there is no regex to have groups. Using
.B $2
when the regex only has one capture group is rejected at load time too,
naming the offending regex and its file:line.
.SH RULE EVALUATION
.IP 1. 4
Rules are tested in the order they appear in the file.
.IP 2.
For each rule the
.B if
condition is evaluated against the current message context.
.IP 3.
If the condition is true, the body block is entered:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B reject
\(em terminates evaluation; message is rejected.
.IP \(bu
.B accept
\(em terminates evaluation; message is accepted.
.IP \(bu
.B warn
\(em logs a warning; evaluation continues with the next top-level rule.
.IP \(bu
.B dunno
\(em no action; evaluation continues with the next top-level rule.
.RE
.IP 4.
If the condition is false the
.BR else / "else if"
chain is tested.
If no branch matches, evaluation advances to the next top-level rule.
.IP 5.
If no rule produces
.B reject
or
.BR accept ,
the message is accepted silently.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Blocking a simple phrase in Subject
.nf
.RS
if subject /YOU\es+PERVERT/i {
    reject "Spam: Email blackmail rejected";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Conditional routing by recipient (invoice routing), infix style
Accept invoice-related messages only for designated mailboxes;
reject all other recipients.
.nf
.RS
if subject /invoice/i {
    if rcpt_to /^<accounting@example\.com>$/i or rcpt_to /^<director@example\.com>$/i {
        accept;
    } else {
        reject "Invoice: unauthorized recipient";
    }
}
.RE
.fi
The same rule, written as a single condition with
.BR and / not :
.nf
.RS
if subject /invoice/i and not (
    rcpt_to /^<accounting@example\.com>$/i or rcpt_to /^<director@example\.com>$/i
) {
    reject "Invoice: unauthorized recipient";
}
.RE
.fi
And the equivalent legacy
.B allof
/
.B anyof
/
.B not
form (all three forms are interchangeable):
.nf
.RS
if allof(
    subject /invoice/i,
    not anyof(
        rcpt_to /^<accounting@example\.com>$/i,
        rcpt_to /^<director@example\.com>$/i
    )
) {
    reject "Invoice: unauthorized recipient";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Blocking Cyrillic subjects
.nf
.RS
if subject /[Ѐ-ӿ]/ {
    reject "Spam: Cyrillic subject rejected";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Blocking emoji, with a sender exception (infix and/not)
Reject messages whose Subject contains emoji, unless they come from
a trusted transactional-mail domain.
.nf
.RS
if subject /[\eU0001F300-\eU0001F9FF\eU0001F004-\eU0001F0CF\eU00002600-\eU000027BF]/
   and not envelope_from /@notifications\.stripo\.email>?$/i {
    reject "Emoji not allowed";
}
.RE
.fi
Note the
.B >?
before the anchor: the envelope address includes the closing angle
bracket, so an anchored pattern must either allow for it or remain
unanchored (see the
.I envelope_from
test description above).
.SS Blocking CJK scripts
.nf
.RS
if subject /[\eu3040-\eu30FF\eu4E00-\eu9FFF\eu3400-\eu4DBF\euF900-\euFAFF\euAC00-\euD7AF\eU00020000-\eU0002A6DF]/ {
    reject "Spam: CJK script subject rejected";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Combining envelope sender and arbitrary header (numeric threshold)
Reject messages that appear to be spam (high X-Spam-Score) with a
suspicious subject, but only from external senders. This assumes the
header carries a plain numeric value \(em verify this against your own
scanner's actual output before relying on it, since some spam scanners
put a per-rule score breakdown under a similarly-named header instead
of a single total (see
.B "Numeric comparisons"
above).
.nf
.RS
if subject /pharmacy|pills|viagra/i
   and not envelope_from /@example\.com>/i
   and header "X-Spam-Score" > 15 {
    reject "Spam: high score + suspicious subject";
}
.RE
.fi
This correctly rejects a score of
.I 1000
(e.g. SpamAssassin's GTUBE test string) just as readily as a score of
.IR 16 .
An older regex-based approach such as
.I "header \(dqX-Spam-Score\(dq /^[89]\ed\./"
only matches two-digit scores in the 80\-99 range \(em it silently
misses both smaller two-digit scores above the threshold (e.g.
.IR 16\-79 )
and any three-or-more-digit score.
.SS warn for monitoring before blocking
.nf
.RS
if subject /invoice/i and envelope_from /@external\.example\.com>/i {
    warn "External invoice -- monitoring";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Verbose regex with comments (x flag)
.nf
.RS
if subject /
    (recipe|reduce|avoid(ing)?|prevent(ing)?)?
    .*
    knee
    .*
    (pain|surger(y|ies)|fail(ing)?)
/ix {
    reject "Knee spam";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS else if chain
.nf
.RS
if envelope_from /newsletter@/i {
    dunno;
} else if subject /[Ѐ-ӿ]/ {
    reject "Cyrillic subject";
} else if subject /[\eu4E00-\eu9FFF]/ {
    reject "CJK subject";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Nested conditions with reply-to check
.nf
.RS
if subject /unsubscribe|opt.out/i
   and not (reply_to /@example\.com>/i or envelope_from /@example\.com>/i) {
    warn "Suspicious unsubscribe from external sender";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Operator-precedence pitfall
Without parentheses,
.B and
binds tighter than
.BR or ,
so the following does
.B NOT
mean \(lqsubject matches X or Y, and the sender must always be
external\(rq:
.nf
.RS
\fR# WRONG \(em accidentally accepts internal senders when subject is X
if subject /X/i or subject /Y/i and not envelope_from /@example\.com>/i {
    reject;
}
.RE
.fi
The correct form groups the
.B or
explicitly:
.nf
.RS
if (subject /X/i or subject /Y/i) and not envelope_from /@example\.com>/i {
    reject;
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Blocking HTML-entity obfuscation in the body
Spammers sometimes encode every character of the body as an HTML entity
.RI ( &#65; " or " &#x41; " for " A )
so that plain-text dictionary filters see nothing readable. Six or more
entities within 100 characters is a strong obfuscation signal:
.nf
.RS
if body /(?:&#(?:\ed{2,6}|x[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,5});.{0,100}){6,}/s {
    reject "HTML entity obfuscation detected";
}
.RE
.fi
.SS Combining Subject and body in one rule (Business Email Compromise)
This is the main scenario that motivated merging the Subject-only and
body-only milters into
.BR mail_filter :
a single rule can now require an urgent-sounding Subject
.B and
a financial-fraud phrase in the body
.B and
an external sender, in one place:
.nf
.RS
if subject /urgent|ASAP|confidential/i
   and (body /wire\es+transfer|bank\es+transfer|IBAN|SWIFT/i
        or body /send\es+funds|transfer\es+funds/i)
   and not envelope_from /@yourcompany\.com>/i {
    reject "Possible BEC -- financial request from external sender";
}
.RE
.fi
.SH TESTING
Test a message context against the current rules without sending mail.
By default the normal system configuration is used. To test an alternate
configuration tree, use
.BR --test-config .
Relative
.B rules
and
.B whitelist
paths inside that configuration are resolved relative to the directory
containing the alternate
.B mail_filter.conf
file; relative
.B refile:
paths are then resolved relative to the selected rules file.
.PP
Test switches are grouped into a Subject/envelope/header group
.RB ( --subject ,
.BR --envelope-from ,
.BR --envelope-to ,
.BR --from ,
.BR --to ,
.BR --header )
and a body group
.RB ( --body " / " --body-file ).
Both groups can be combined freely in one
.B --test
invocation to exercise a rule that requires both a
.B subject
and a
.B body
condition, e.g.\& a Business-Email-Compromise style rule. Within the
body group,
.B --body
and
.B --body-file
are mutually exclusive and cannot be given together.
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Quick subject test
mail_filter --test --subject "Invoice 2024" \e
    --envelope-from "<supplier@external-domain.com>" \e
    --envelope-to "<accounting@example.com>"

# Quick body test
mail_filter --test --body "please wire the payment to our new IBAN"

# Combined subject + body test (e.g. a BEC-style rule)
mail_filter --test \e
    --subject "Urgent payment request" \e
    --body "Please process a wire transfer today" \e
    --envelope-from "<ceo@external-domain.com>"

# With From/To headers
mail_filter --test \e
    --subject "Invoice #42" \e
    --envelope-from "<a@b.com>" \e
    --envelope-to "<finance@example.com>" \e
    --from "A B <a@b.com>" \e
    --header "X-Spam-Score: 3.2"

# Testing a numeric-comparison rule (see CONDITIONS / Numeric comparisons):
# a rule with `header "X-Spam-Score-Total" > 15 { reject; }` fires on this
mail_filter --test --header "X-Spam-Score-Total: 23.5"

# ...but not on this (below the threshold)
mail_filter --test --header "X-Spam-Score-Total: 3.2"

# Body from a file
mail_filter --test --body-file /tmp/suspect-body.txt

# From a JSON context file (subject and body together)
mail_filter --test --test-context /tmp/testmsg.json

# Test a self-contained alternate configuration directory
mail_filter --test --test-config /tmp/mail_filter-test/mail_filter.conf \e
    --subject "Invoice 2024"

# Test an entire EML file (subject, body and MIME parts are extracted
# automatically; --subject/--body/--test-context are ignored if also given)
mail_filter --test-eml /tmp/suspect-message.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
The JSON file format used by
.B --test-context
carries both the Subject and body in a single context, together with the
envelope and headers:
.nf
.RS
{
  "subject": "Invoice 2024",
  "body": "Please wire the outstanding balance to our new account.",
  "envelope_from": "<supplier@external-domain.com>",
  "envelope_to": ["<accounting@example.com>", "<director@example.com>"],
  "headers": {
    "From": "Dodavatel <supplier@external-domain.com>",
    "X-Spam-Score": "2.1"
  }
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Conflicting test switches.
.BR --configtest ,
.BR --test-eml ,
and
.B --test-context
each build their own, self-contained message context and take priority
over any Subject/envelope/header or body switches given alongside them.
When such a conflicting combination is detected, mail_filter prints a
.B WARN
line to standard error naming the switches that were ignored, then
proceeds using the higher-priority option; it does not abort. Combining
plain
.BR --subject / --envelope-* / --from / --to / --header
switches with plain
.B --body / --body-file
switches is
.B not
a conflict \(em that is the normal way to test a rule spanning both the
Subject and the body.
.PP
Exit codes:
.B 0
= ACCEPT,
.B 1
= REJECT.
.PP
On a match, the tester also prints the exact chain of rule lines
that led to the result (top-level
.BR if ,
any nested
.BR if s,
down to the terminating action), so you can see precisely which
rule \(em and which branch of it \(em fired.
